#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9611562c-7b6a-5fd7-b4a4-8b909569962f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284219} {:answer/id #uuid "28f5f1a5-f068-5698-8912-f4cf6b923f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284220} {:answer/id #uuid "a22b9d80-841f-5778-84d6-4f7c8885f87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x<\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284221} {:answer/id #uuid "7618b474-e6f0-5c09-8fc1-46f7827e714b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}<x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284222} {:answer/id #uuid "2f04df1c-d3b2-5364-bb89-a2e0e96377dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7618b474-e6f0-5c09-8fc1-46f7827e714b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}<x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284222} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc7202c-f285-5f70-9dce-26b35116840b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e98cc8a-a07a-5c51-9aa1-e06510c614aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-102975" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5)-2\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5)-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5) & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5)} & \\le {e}^{2} \\\\ 3x+5 & \\le {e}^{2} \\\\ 3x & \\le {e}^{2}-5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n3x+5>0\\,⇒\\,x>-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-\\frac{5}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{5}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}]\\cap (-\\frac{5}{3},\\infty )=(-\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{5}{3}<x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "262126df-a114-500d-87d4-491e46c43b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e\\le x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284226} {:answer/id #uuid "0fffda1e-cf30-52eb-8f5b-b68537b0b2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284227} {:answer/id #uuid "25a16f5d-267f-55da-a0eb-959002ab150c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284228} {:answer/id #uuid "2e30063e-67ec-5a13-9f9f-6ad0d26bca58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284229} {:answer/id #uuid "f5193c01-d10c-5900-835d-48ce0b98a7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 4+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e30063e-67ec-5a13-9f9f-6ad0d26bca58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284229} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf3f98f-2491-512f-b9cc-4a8adf2e0ef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3da04af-bb57-590e-a310-51cf6887be50" :question/math-academy-id "q-102977" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)-1\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x) & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)} & \\ge {e}^{1} \\\\ 4-x & \\ge e \\\\ -x & \\ge e-4 \\\\ x & \\le 4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n4-x>0\\,⇒\\,x<4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 4-e,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4-e].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<4,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,4-e]\\cap (-\\infty ,4)=(-\\infty ,4-e]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,4-e],$ which can also be written as $x\\le 4-e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c599bfdf-f878-533f-9611-490f851505d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284233} {:answer/id #uuid "66116d64-138c-5c2e-b4d3-02b984128a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x<\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284234} {:answer/id #uuid "3979e35e-0fa7-5506-92e0-22d710ab9d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284235} {:answer/id #uuid "a65334c7-6ba5-5e6c-93a0-b979dff85451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}<x\\le \\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284236} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6b6c25-120d-5563-a6e2-66393b2f535f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6b6c25-120d-5563-a6e2-66393b2f535f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284237} :answer-field/id #uuid "a737e259-6b56-586c-8338-68260c22934f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fe7dba8-e1c9-5093-ae4a-b44af700b7b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-102979" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(4x-1)\\ge {log}_{2}⁡(1-\\frac{x}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x-1)} & \\ge {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(1-x/4)} \\\\ 4x-1 & \\ge 1-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ \\frac{17x}{4} & \\ge 2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{x}{4} & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{8}{17},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{8}{17},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x<4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )$ and $(-\\infty ,4)$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{8}{17},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,4)=[\\frac{8}{17},4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{8}{17},4),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d372c7d-9ae5-500f-a429-6653130faec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d372c7d-9ae5-500f-a429-6653130faec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284240} :answer-field/id #uuid "f13a9a01-5279-53e0-92b6-e691eb6405c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284239} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea91dc15-097b-59c1-93d1-5304bb4577fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea91dc15-097b-59c1-93d1-5304bb4577fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284242} :answer-field/id #uuid "aadaf901-3c96-5f5d-aa0c-a8b7775ddcdc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb6f2386-8767-561f-ad3a-bba78785d278" :question/math-academy-id "q-278673" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{5}⁡(6x-3)\\ge {log}_{5}⁡(3x+9).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $5.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x-3)} & \\ge {5}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+9)} \\\\ 6x-3 & \\ge 3x+9 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 12 \\\\ x & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+9 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 4,$ which can be expressed as $[4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x>-3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ and $(-3,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[4,\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=[4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327f4ddf-a8a9-59f2-894e-49a8d40e3c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3<x\\le \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284245} {:answer/id #uuid "e081b485-b1f9-54f3-9f36-378f7be65630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284246} {:answer/id #uuid "a38c851a-4e44-534e-b5f1-5b3d8cbb114b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284247} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9d6572-64a3-5b86-ab42-922026a330cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x\\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284248} {:answer/id #uuid "819fb781-93bd-5fd7-851b-44bc108d9e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x\\le \\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e081b485-b1f9-54f3-9f36-378f7be65630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284246} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efa13e9-8f54-52a8-bd3e-5cb669a7e6c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63e61ca1-883d-5b21-b2c1-7fcd88f8bb68" :question/math-academy-id "q-88024" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(x+3)+4\\le {log}_{2}⁡(x-2).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+3)+4} & \\le {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+3)}\\cdot {2}^{4} & \\le {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ (x+3)\\cdot 16 & \\le x-2 \\\\ 16x+48 & \\le x-2 \\\\ 15x & \\le -50 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3 \\\\ x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le -\\frac{10}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{10}{3}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-3$ and $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,\\infty )$ and $(2,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{10}{3}]\\cap (-3,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution."}]}
